      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we need to complete the square for both the \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) terms.\n\nFirst, consider the \\( x \\)-terms: \\( x^2 + 6x \\). To complete the square, we add and subtract \\( (6/2)^2 = 9 \\):\n\\[ x^2 + 6x = (x + 3)^2 - 9. \\]\n\nNext, consider the \\( y \\)-terms: \\( 2y^2 - 4y \\). To complete the square, we factor out the 2 first and then complete the square for \\( y^2 - 2y \\):\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y = 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y - 1)^2 - 1) = 2(y - 1)^2 - 2. \\]\n\nNow, substitute these completed squares back into the original expression for \\( z \\):\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 - 9 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 + 22. \\]\nCombine the constant terms:\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11. \\]\n\nThe expression \\( (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 \\) is a sum of two squares, both of which are always non-negative and achieve their minimum value of 0 when their arguments are 0. Therefore, the minimum value of \\( z \\) occurs when both \\( (x + 3)^2 = 0 \\) and \\( 2(y - 1)^2 = 0 \\), which happens when \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\).\n\nSubstitute \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\) into the expression for \\( z \\):\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11 = 0 + 0 + 11 = 11. \\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
