      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we will complete the square for both \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) terms.\n\nFirst, consider the \\( x \\)-terms:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x. \\]\nTo complete the square, we take half of the coefficient of \\( x \\), which is 6, divide by 2 to get 3, and then square it to get 9. So we can rewrite \\( x^2 + 6x \\) as:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x = (x + 3)^2 - 9. \\]\n\nNext, consider the \\( y \\)-terms:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y. \\]\nWe factor out the 2 from the \\( y \\)-terms:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y = 2(y^2 - 2y). \\]\nTo complete the square inside the parentheses, we take half of the coefficient of \\( y \\), which is -2, divide by 2 to get -1, and then square it to get 1. So we can rewrite \\( y^2 - 2y \\) as:\n\\[ y^2 - 2y = (y - 1)^2 - 1. \\]\nTherefore,\n\\[ 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y - 1)^2 - 1) = 2(y - 1)^2 - 2. \\]\n\nNow, substitute these completed squares back into the original expression for \\( z \\):\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 - 9 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 + 22. \\]\nSimplify the constants:\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11. \\]\n\nSince \\((x + 3)^2 \\geq 0\\) and \\(2(y - 1)^2 \\geq 0\\) for all real numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), the minimum value of \\(z\\) occurs when both \\((x + 3)^2 = 0\\) and \\(2(y - 1)^2 = 0\\), which happens when \\(x = -3\\) and \\(y = 1\\). Substituting these values into the expression for \\(z\\), we get:\n\\[ z = 0 + 0 + 11 = 11. \\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\(z\\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
